INT. MINIATURE-GOLF CLOCK TOWER, WINDMILL HOLE - NIGHT

A faded mini-golf course after closing. Plastic windmill, fiberglass lighthouse, a clock-tower hole with a hollow base. A service panel stands open at its foot.

A figure in a black hoodie crouches inside the clock tower with a headlamp and a duffel bag. A TALL MAN (40s, careful hands, wedding ring on a chain around his neck) works a thin pick into the panel's lock.

A second figure appears at the fence line. A WOMAN (30s, sneakers, a tote bag from a bakery) climbs over, lands badly, and freezes. They stare at each other.

The Tall Man lifts one finger to his lips. She nods. Walks over without a sound, sets down the bakery tote, and kneels beside him.

WOMAN
(whispering)
You're late.

TALL MAN
You're early.

WOMAN
There's a cop at the corner.

TALL MAN
He's eating.

WOMAN
How do you know he's eating?

TALL MAN
Because it's a Tuesday and Tuesdays are pierogi.

She considers this. Looks at the lock. At his hands.

WOMAN
You said four minutes.

He does not answer. The pick clicks. Stops. He pulls it out, wipes it on his sleeve, and starts again, slower.

WOMAN (CONT'D)
(to herself)
Okay. Okay. Okay.

TALL MAN
Talk to the lock, not me.

WOMAN
The lock doesn't care.

TALL MAN
Neither do I. Please.

A siren, distant, passes the block and fades. Neither of them breathes until it does. He lowers his headlamp to a dim red.

The lock gives with a soft thunk. He pulls the panel back. Inside, a steel box the size of a toaster.

WOMAN
That's it?

TALL MAN
That's it.

WOMAN
Thirty years in this city and the most valuable thing in it is under a clock tower with a cartoon face.

TALL MAN
Dolores, that's the point.

The woman, DOLORES, goes still. He realizes what he's said. Neither speaks.

DOLORES
You said you wouldn't use names.

TALL MAN
I know.

DOLORES
So you know mine.

He closes his eyes briefly. Opens them. Reaches for the steel box and lifts it out with both hands. It's heavier than it looks.

TALL MAN
Walt.

DOLORES
Walt.

WALT
My name. Since we're doing it.

She takes the box from him and puts it in the bakery tote under a layer of paper-wrapped rolls. A long, satisfied, silent beat. Then headlights sweep the course.

DOLORES
(low)
Walt.

WALT
I see it.

They crouch lower inside the hollow of the tower, shoulders pressed together. The lights pass across the fiberglass lighthouse. Stop. Linger.

A car door opens.

WALT (CONT'D)
(whispering)
Pierogi's done early.

DOLORES
Don't joke.

WALT
I'm not.

She takes his hand. He lets her.
